The Hesperid is the ship of the company that built [[Geography/Færrin/Infinite Horizon/Far Realms/Hesperid 3|Hesperid 3]] and its fellows Hesperid 1 and Hesperid 2, and the base the company called HQ. It lies on its side on the gray waste at the north end of the rail, a hull miles long with its top shorn away and its floors open to the air, stranded where it lies. The company's outposts were set to mine and refine fuel for it.

It is no longer one hull. The ship has been copied six times over around a point in the air and turns through itself in place, pieces swapping and overlapping like a kaleidoscope, so that a single structure shows as six, one for each rail that circles it. A beat sounds as it turns, and something alive is behind the turning: the presence [[Org/Sedecium|Sedecium]] calls [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Esoterica|Gremory]], a fourth-generation Thessian. A Sedecium crew found him here on the eve of his ascent and ran their ship into him to stop it; the blow cut the ship nearly in half and failed, and his beat has turned the wreck since. The six-fold working came later, and the same pattern shows on Hesperid 3.

The rail enters what was a cargo hold, now folded. The tarmac road turns a right angle and runs straight up the wall, cones and vehicles stuck flat to it, its painted stripes carrying on across the ceiling; a body that steps onto a wall turns with it, and can walk up and stand on the ceiling. A loading arm reaches into an arriving train for its barrel, and a shack of salvaged board and corrugated metal beside the dock keeps the forms for what comes and goes. A six-wheeled pallet with a barrel rack and no motor or controls sits at the dock.

At the end of the road is an open elevator shaft lined with skulls, turning with the beat. From a great door at its foot pours a sickly iridescent light, the color of the glow over [[Geography/Færrin/Calaria/index|Calaria]] gone wrong. Between the bones are speaker grates that hiss; a listener who puts an ear to one can pick a voice out of the static warning them to get somewhere else. The light has fallen on the shaft for decades, and its metal struts have come alive as [[Bestiary/Constructs|living incantations]]. The working is a curse past any mortal hand, a god's power turned to a wrong use, and beyond the door lies the engine room at the wreck's heart, where Gremory sits in his own body.

Below the cargo hold, a maintenance level carries the ship’s piping and conduit, its halls twisted so that a walker keeps their footing while the floor rolls and folds around them. The cargo elevator serves four floors: the hold, the maintenance level beneath it, a third-floor lobby that keeps its gold inlay, booths and plants though the ship has folded parts of it away, and the fourth floor, the bridge. The bridge holds the helm, a wide room of glass and consoles, and behind a great metal door a shelter the crew called the safe room, where non-combatants were meant to muster. In the copy dated 112, one man still lived sealed in that shelter, the Nephilim researcher [[People/Oliver Day|Oliver Day]]. A hallway off the helm runs back into the ship to a research lab of long desks, computers, radar dishes and petri dishes. The shelter and the lab each stand behind a door with a panel beside it, cut with an indentation for Sedecium's sixteen-pointed key. In the earliest copies the bridge is crushed and the helm with it; in later ones a way through opens.

Off the art-deco spine of hallways where the ship entertained visitors, two lengths are still intact, one to a canteen and one to a dormitory. The dormitory is a long arc of numbered bunk rooms, 101 to 128, and it took the brunt of the collision with Gremory; most of its rooms are sheared open or collapsed. At its aft end, nearest the engine bell, two larger rooms survived. The first is the captain's quarters, hung with a jacket and cap in the style of a Færrin ship's master, its desk holding a travel log of close to three years chasing the Thessian and a console that shows the company's map of the [[Geography/index|Astra]]: a spiral of stars with their own position marked near the center, and a region of some forty stars fenced off and stamped with the sixteen-pointed star of Sedecium, running from the rim to near the middle, its far ends labeled [[Geography/Ushas/index|Ushas]] and [[Geography/Althane/index|Althane]]. The crew's logs follow leads they called the entity, which brought them to a station that had seen a burst of energy near the center, labeled the Astra. The second room, an officer's, held a nightstand of eight enchanted pieces shaped like spiked stars, each carrying a fraction of one long prime number; a full set of sixteen would lock together into a rendition of Sedecium's sixteen-pointed star and turn as a key, and more of the pieces were scattered when the room was sheared.

The canteen is a mess hall of long tables with their benches joined in, its pots, packets and rations floating untethered. Every six seconds a beat sounds there, and with each beat the room swaps through the ship's other copies: one where it is burnt, one where its furniture has crushed into a single piece, one where it is gone. A body that keeps hold of floor, wall or ceiling stays where it is; one floating when the beat comes slips into another copy and vanishes from the room it left.

The stations along the rail loop stand at different times: the years 72, 82, 94, 112, 122 and 142. Three barrels of fuel had come in at the station dated 72, one at 94, and five at 142.
